 "intro": "The Hanseatic League, or Hansa, was a network of German merchant guilds and trading towns that dominated the sea trade of the Baltic and the North Sea from the thirteenth to the fifteenth century. Led by Lübeck, it carried furs, wax, grain, timber and fish west and cloth and salt east, and kept privileged trading houses, the Kontore, at Novgorod, Bergen, Bruges and London. It never had a standing army, a treasury or a permanent government, yet in 1370 it forced the king of Denmark to terms. This map follows its main lanes from the alliance of Lübeck and Hamburg to the closing of the London Steelyard.",
 "outcome": "The Hansa peaked after the Peace of Stralsund in 1370, then lost ground to Dutch shipping and territorial princes; its last diet met in 1669.",
 "uncertainty": "The sea lanes are drawn through named straits and capes to show which towns traded with which, not the courses ships steered, and goods are listed only where the sources name them. The Hansa had no fixed membership, so the towns shown are a selection. Grain volumes are given only for the Vistula, the one route with a figure in the sources used. The founding date is a convention: the word Hanse appears in 1267, and a general diet first met around 1356, though some place the first diets around 1300.",
