   "id": "lubeck-hamburg",
   "title": "Lübeck and Hamburg join hands",
   "time": "1241",
   "narrative": "By the early thirteenth century German merchants sailing from Lübeck had joined the Gotlanders of Visby in the trade to Novgorod, the great market of the Russian north, where they kept a trading yard of their own, the Peterhof. In 1241 Lübeck, with its harbour on the Baltic and its fishing grounds, allied with Hamburg, which commanded the routes carrying salt from Lüneburg. The short land link between the two towns let goods pass from one sea to the other without sailing round Denmark.",
