  {
   "id": "kontore",
   "title": "Kontore and a diet",
   "time": "1347–1356",
   "narrative": "By the middle of the fourteenth century the four great foreign trading houses, the Kontore at Novgorod, Bergen, Bruges and London, were organised corporations with their own treasuries, courts and seals. At Bergen the Germans traded grain for dried stockfish, and in Scania their seasonal stations salted the herring of the Sound. In 1347 the Bruges Kontor divided its members into three regional thirds, and in 1356 a diet of the towns met at Lübeck. The Black Death had just struck many of the towns in 1349 and 1350.",
