   "id": "novgorod-closed",
   "title": "Ivan III closes the Peterhof",
   "time": "1494",
   "narrative": "In 1494 Ivan III of Moscow, master of Novgorod since 1478, closed the Peterhof and sent its merchants, only 49 by then, to Moscow. The fur trade turned toward Leipzig and the Russian business moved to Riga, Reval and Pskov; when the yard reopened in 1514, Novgorod was no longer a great market. In the south Baltic, grain from Poland now came down the Vistula to Danzig, about 10,000 short tons a year by the late fifteenth century.",
